  "worked_solution": "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${t}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{{t}_{3}}={{(2)}_{\u23df}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{{t}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{\u23df}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{{t}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$",
